  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Naming the Application**\n\nI\'ve decided to name the application \"DealPlate.\" I\'m also updating the description within `metadata.json` using `edit_file`. I\'m going to take a look at the image files that are available, now that I\'ve finalized the name. It appears that there are a few.\n\n\n**Analyzing Screenflows**\n\nI\'m now examining the key screen flows within the application. I\'ve broken it down to Registration, Verification, and the Marketplace. Registration captures user details like Full Name, Phone Number, and University Email. Verification involves selecting a University and uploading a Student ID. The Marketplace includes Explore, Orders and Profile options. I\'m focusing on the user journey and data flow here.\n\n\n**Defining Deal Listing Components**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the components of a deal listing. I\'m breaking down the elements such as the \"50-70% OFF\" badge, the title, vendor and campus info, time slot, pricing in Ksh, and the shopping bag button. I\'m also examining the structure of the Sign In screen flow, the Deal Details page, and the Checkout process and its constituent elements.\n\n\n**Detailing Order Confirmation**\n\nI\'m now detailing the Order Confirmation screen. It has a checkmark icon and the message \"Order Confirmed! Your flash deal is secured.\" A pickup code box, with text \"DP-X792K\", and a QR code will be shown. I\'ll include a countdown timer for the pickup window and helper text: \"Show this to the cashier\".\n\n**Defining Deal Payment Flow**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the Deal Payment Flow. I\'m noting the Item Card details: image, a discount, and name. The payment method involves an M-Pesa number field and an order summary showing subtotal, fees, and the total in orange. A green button indicates payment via M-Pesa. I\'m adding helper text for clarity on the payment process.\n\n**Describing the Student Profile**\n\nNow, I\'m describing the Student Profile screen. There\'s a top bar with the user\'s name, profile image, edit button, and a \"Verified Student Status\" badge. It includes a stats grid for \"Total Saved\" and \"Meals Enjoyed.\" Past orders are listed with order cards showing details. Bottom navigation includes \"Explore\", \"Orders\", and \"Profile\" options.\n\n\n**Defining Interactive Prototyping**\n\nI\'m now outlining a high-fidelity interactive prototype, seeing the current screens as an interactive flow with React state. I\'ll include form submissions, step transitions with Framer Motion, a real-time countdown timer, and a simulated M-Pesa transaction with latency. I\'ll use local storage for mock data and progress. I\'m also going to begin to check the design rules and enforce the Tailwind color palette. I\'ll be sure to use `#FF6B00`, `#E11D48`, and `#F3F4F6` for our primary actions, urgency, and surfaces, respectively, sticking to the styling guidelines.\n\n\n**Defining Design Rules**\n\nI\'m now detailing the application\'s design rules and typography conventions. I\'ve defined the color palette, using primary orange `#FF6B00`, urgency red `#E11D48`, and surface colors `#F3F4F6` or `#fff8f6` for backgrounds, with white `#FFFFFF` for the lowest container. I\'m also planning to use `#111827` for high-contrast primary text and `#5a4136` or `#8e7164` for subtle secondary text. I\'ve established typography rules using `Plus Jakarta Sans` for headers & prices and `Inter` for body text and labels, with clear formatting for prices in Ksh. Button components will use a minimum touch target size of 48px and rounded corners.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will update `metadata.json` to name the app \"DealPlate\" and write an accurate description that aligns with its high-velocity flash deal aesthetic for Nairobi university students."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 319739791
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
